select 'bbbb0001-0000-4000-8000-000000000013', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The conditional probability P(A|B) is defined as...","options":["P(A) * P(B)","P(A and B) / P(B)","P(A and B) / P(A)","P(B) / P(A)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"P(A|B) = P(A and B) / P(B): you restrict attention to the world where B happened and ask how much of it also has A."},{"prompt":"Bayes'' theorem is mainly used to...","options":["turn P(D|H) into P(H|D)","prove two events are independent","compute an average","sort a data set"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bayes'' theorem flips a conditional: it converts the likelihood P(D|H) into the posterior P(H|D)."},{"prompt":"A prior probability represents...","options":["belief after seeing the data","belief about a hypothesis before seeing the data","the likelihood of the data","the size of the sample"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The prior encodes what you believe about the hypothesis before this evidence arrives."},{"prompt":"The likelihood P(D|H) is...","options":["the probability of the hypothesis","the probability of the data given the hypothesis","the prior","the posterior"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The likelihood asks: if this hypothesis were true, how probable would the observed data be?"},{"prompt":"By the law of total probability, P(D) can be written as...","options":["P(D|H) * P(H)","P(D|H) * P(H) + P(D|not H) * P(not H)","P(H|D)","P(D) + P(H)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You split D across every way it can happen: through H and through not-H, weighting each by its probability."},{"prompt":"Two events A and B are independent exactly when...","options":["P(A and B) = P(A) * P(B)","P(A|B) = 0","they cannot both happen","P(A) = P(B)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independence means the joint probability factors: P(A and B) = P(A) * P(B), equivalently P(A|B) = P(A)."},{"prompt":"A 95 percent credible interval is best described as...","options":["a range that holds the parameter with probability 0.95","a p-value","a single best guess","a range that is always symmetric"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In the Bayesian view the parameter has a distribution, so a credible interval is a range that contains it with the stated probability."},{"prompt":"If the observed data are equally probable under every hypothesis, the posterior...","options":["doubles the prior","equals the prior","becomes 1","becomes 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When the likelihood is the same for all hypotheses, it cancels in Bayes'' theorem and the data leave your belief unchanged."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Probability review and conditional probability', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Compute joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities, apply the multiplication rule, and use the law of total probability. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what does the bar in P(A|B) mean?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A probability is a number between 0 and 1 measuring how likely an event is. The joint probability P(A and B) is the chance both happen. The conditional probability P(A|B) = P(A and B) / P(B) narrows the world down to outcomes where B occurred and asks what fraction of them also have A. Rearranging gives the multiplication rule P(A and B) = P(A|B) * P(B) = P(B|A) * P(A). Events are independent when P(A|B) = P(A), which is the same as P(A and B) = P(A) * P(B). Finally, the law of total probability rebuilds a marginal probability from conditional pieces: P(D) = P(D|H) * P(H) + P(D|not H) * P(not H)."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A standard deck has 52 cards. P(king) = 4/52 = 1/13. Draw one card; given that it is a face card (12 of them), P(king | face card) = P(king and face) / P(face) = (4/52) / (12/52) = 4/12 = 1/3. Multiplication rule, drawing two kings without replacement: P(both kings) = P(first king) * P(second king | first king) = (4/52) * (3/51) = 12/2652 = 1/221. Total probability: a factory gets 60 percent of parts from supplier X (2 percent defective) and 40 percent from supplier Y (5 percent defective). Then P(defective) = 0.02 * 0.60 + 0.05 * 0.40 = 0.012 + 0.020 = 0.032, or 3.2 percent."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A fair die is rolled. What is P(roll is 4 | roll is even)? 2) If P(A) = 0.3, P(B) = 0.5, and A, B are independent, what is P(A and B)? 3) A bag has 3 red and 2 blue marbles. Draw two without replacement; what is P(both red)? 4) With the factory above, what fraction of all defective parts came from supplier Y? Answers: 1) Evens are {2,4,6}; P(4 | even) = (1/6) / (3/6) = 1/3. 2) 0.3 * 0.5 = 0.15. 3) (3/5) * (2/4) = 6/20 = 3/10. 4) 0.020 / 0.032 = 0.625, so 62.5 percent - a preview of Bayes'' theorem."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: confusing P(A|B) with P(B|A) - the probability a face card is a king (1/3) is not the probability a king is a face card (1). Mistake: assuming independence and multiplying when the draws actually affect each other. Exit: (a) Write the multiplication rule for P(A and B). (b) State the law of total probability for two cases H and not-H. Answers: P(A and B) = P(A|B) * P(B); P(D) = P(D|H) * P(H) + P(D|not H) * P(not H)."}]}'::jsonb, 'Read P(A|B) as ''the probability of A given B'' - the bar means ''assuming the thing on the right already happened,'' not division.', 13),
  (3, 'Bayes'' theorem intuition', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Derive Bayes'' theorem from the definition of conditional probability and use it to flip P(D|H) into P(H|D). Warm-up: in the last lesson, 62.5 percent of defective parts came from supplier Y even though Y makes only 40 percent of parts - why did that number jump?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Because P(H and D) can be written two ways, P(H|D) * P(D) = P(D|H) * P(H), we can solve for the quantity we usually want. Bayes'' theorem is P(H|D) = P(D|H) * P(H) / P(D). In words: the posterior P(H|D) equals the likelihood P(D|H) times the prior P(H), divided by the total probability of the data P(D). The denominator is computed with the law of total probability, P(D) = P(D|H) * P(H) + P(D|not H) * P(not H), and its only job is to make the posterior probabilities add to 1. The lesson of the theorem is that a rare hypothesis stays fairly improbable even after supportive evidence, because the prior P(H) sits right there in the numerator."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A disease affects 1 percent of people, so P(H) = 0.01 and P(not H) = 0.99. A test detects it 99 percent of the time when present, P(D|H) = 0.99, but also gives a positive on 5 percent of healthy people, P(D|not H) = 0.05. You test positive; what is P(H|D)? First the denominator: P(D) = P(D|H) * P(H) + P(D|not H) * P(not H) = 0.99 * 0.01 + 0.05 * 0.99 = 0.0099 + 0.0495 = 0.0594. Then Bayes'' theorem: P(H|D) = P(D|H) * P(H) / P(D) = 0.0099 / 0.0594 = 0.1667, about 16.7 percent. Even after a positive from a very accurate test, it is more likely than not that you are healthy - because the disease was rare to begin with, the 0.0495 stream of false positives outweighs the 0.0099 stream of true positives."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Keep P(D|H) = 0.99 and P(D|not H) = 0.05, but raise prevalence to P(H) = 0.10. Compute P(D), then P(H|D). 2) In the original 1 percent example, what is P(not H | D)? 3) If a test had P(D|not H) = 0 (no false positives), what would a positive imply about H? Answers: 1) P(D) = 0.99 * 0.10 + 0.05 * 0.90 = 0.099 + 0.045 = 0.144; P(H|D) = 0.099 / 0.144 = 0.6875, about 68.8 percent - a higher prior lifts the posterior sharply. 2) 1 - 0.1667 = 0.8333, about 83.3 percent. 3) With P(D|not H) = 0, a positive can only come from H, so P(H|D) = 1."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: base-rate neglect - reading the 99 percent test accuracy as the chance you are sick and ignoring the 1 percent prior. Mistake: forgetting the false-positive stream when computing P(D). Exit: (a) Write Bayes'' theorem. (b) In the 1 percent example, why is the posterior only 16.7 percent despite a 99 percent accurate test? Answers: P(H|D) = P(D|H) * P(H) / P(D); because the disease is rare, false positives from the 99 percent of healthy people outnumber true positives from the 1 percent who are sick."}]}'::jsonb, 'Bayes'' theorem just re-expresses the conditional probability definition; the whole formula follows from P(A|B) = P(A and B) / P(B) applied twice.', 15),
  (4, 'Priors, likelihood, and posterior', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Name the three moving parts of Bayes'' theorem, explain what each contributes, and use the proportional form to compare hypotheses without computing the denominator. Warm-up: which term in Bayes'' theorem carries what you believed before the data?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Every Bayesian calculation has the same three ingredients. The prior P(H) is your belief before the data. The likelihood P(D|H) says how well each hypothesis predicts the observed data. The posterior P(H|D) is your updated belief. Bayes'' theorem multiplies prior by likelihood and then normalizes: P(H|D) = P(D|H) * P(H) / P(D). Because P(D) does not depend on which hypothesis you are scoring, it is the same divisor for all of them, so posterior ~ P(D|H) * P(H). This proportional form is the workhorse: compute likelihood times prior for each hypothesis, then divide each by the sum to normalize. The likelihood is not a probability distribution over hypotheses; it is read across hypotheses for fixed data, and the numbers need not add to 1 until you normalize."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Two hypotheses for a coin: H1 fair (P(heads) = 0.5) and H2 biased (P(heads) = 0.8). Prior P(H1) = 0.5, P(H2) = 0.5. You flip once and see heads. Unnormalized scores: H1 gives P(D|H1) * P(H1) = 0.5 * 0.5 = 0.25; H2 gives P(D|H2) * P(H2) = 0.8 * 0.5 = 0.40. The normalizer is P(D) = 0.25 + 0.40 = 0.65. Posteriors: P(H1|D) = 0.25 / 0.65 = 0.3846 and P(H2|D) = 0.40 / 0.65 = 0.6154. One heads nudged belief toward the biased coin, from 0.5 to about 0.615. Notice we could also just read the ratio 0.25 to 0.40, i.e. 5 to 8, and normalize: 5/13 = 0.3846 and 8/13 = 0.6154 - same answer, denominator never computed separately."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Same two coins and priors, but now you flip twice and see heads then heads. Compute the unnormalized score for each (use P(HH|H1) = 0.25 and P(HH|H2) = 0.64), then the posteriors. 2) In one line, say what the prior, likelihood, and posterior each mean. 3) Why can the two likelihood values 0.5 and 0.8 fail to add to 1? Answers: 1) H1: 0.25 * 0.5 = 0.125; H2: 0.64 * 0.5 = 0.32; P(D) = 0.445; P(H1|D) = 0.125 / 0.445 = 0.281, P(H2|D) = 0.32 / 0.445 = 0.719. 2) Prior = belief before data; likelihood = how well a hypothesis predicts the data; posterior = updated belief after data. 3) They are probabilities of the data under different hypotheses, not a distribution over hypotheses, so they have no reason to sum to 1."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating the likelihood as a probability of the hypothesis rather than of the data. Mistake: forgetting to normalize, leaving ''posteriors'' that do not sum to 1. Exit: (a) Write the proportional form of Bayes'' theorem. (b) After seeing one heads, which coin became more probable and roughly by how much? Answers: posterior ~ likelihood * prior; the biased coin, rising from 0.5 to about 0.615."}]}'::jsonb, 'Posterior is proportional to likelihood times prior: posterior ~ P(D|H) * P(H). The denominator only rescales, so ratios of posteriors equal ratios of likelihood times prior.', 14),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Given a face card was drawn, the probability it is a king is...","options":["4/52","1/3","1/13","12/52"],"answer":1,"explanation":"P(king | face) = (4/52) / (12/52) = 4/12 = 1/3."},{"prompt":"Bayes'' theorem states P(H|D) equals...","options":["P(D|H) * P(H) / P(D)","P(H|D) * P(D)","P(D) / P(H)","P(D|H) + P(H)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The posterior is likelihood times prior over the total probability of the data."},{"prompt":"In the 1 percent disease example with a positive test, P(H|D) came out to about...","options":["99 percent","50 percent","16.7 percent","5 percent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"0.0099 / 0.0594 = 0.1667; a rare disease keeps the posterior low despite an accurate test."},{"prompt":"The term P(D) in Bayes'' theorem serves to...","options":["carry the prior belief","predict the data from a hypothesis","normalize the posterior so probabilities sum to 1","measure independence"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The denominator is the total probability of the data and rescales the numerators to a valid distribution."},{"prompt":"Two fair-vs-biased coins with equal priors give one heads; the posterior for the biased (0.8) coin is...","options":["0.5","0.3846","0.6154","0.8"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Scores 0.25 and 0.40 normalize to 0.40 / 0.65 = 0.6154."},{"prompt":"The proportional form posterior ~ likelihood * prior is useful because...","options":["it removes the prior","you can compare hypotheses without computing P(D) separately","it makes the likelihood a probability","it only works for coins"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since P(D) is a shared divisor, ratios of likelihood times prior already rank the hypotheses; you normalize at the end."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Discrete Bayesian updating', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Update a discrete set of hypotheses with a table, and chain updates so today''s posterior becomes tomorrow''s prior. Warm-up: if you have three candidate coins, how many rows will your update table have?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"When there are finitely many hypotheses, updating is bookkeeping. Build a table: one row per hypothesis, with columns for the prior P(H), the likelihood P(D|H) of the observed data, their product P(D|H) * P(H), and the posterior. Sum the product column to get P(D); divide each product by that sum to fill the posterior column. The posteriors always add to 1. The deepest habit in Bayesian work is sequential updating: after the first observation, use the posterior as the prior for the next observation. Because independent data multiply their likelihoods, updating once with all the data or updating one datum at a time gives the same final answer."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Three coins, chosen at random so each prior is 1/3: C1 fair P(heads) = 0.5, C2 biased P(heads) = 0.75, C3 two-headed P(heads) = 1.0. You flip once and get heads. Products prior*likelihood: C1 = (1/3) * 0.5 = 0.1667; C2 = (1/3) * 0.75 = 0.2500; C3 = (1/3) * 1.0 = 0.3333. Total P(D) = 0.1667 + 0.2500 + 0.3333 = 0.75. Posteriors: C1 = 0.1667 / 0.75 = 0.2222; C2 = 0.2500 / 0.75 = 0.3333; C3 = 0.3333 / 0.75 = 0.4444 (they sum to 1). Now flip the same coin again and get heads a second time, using these posteriors as the new prior. New products: C1 = 0.2222 * 0.5 = 0.1111; C2 = 0.3333 * 0.75 = 0.2500; C3 = 0.4444 * 1.0 = 0.4444. Total = 0.8055. Posteriors after two heads: C1 = 0.1379; C2 = 0.3103; C3 = 0.5517. Each heads pushes belief toward the two-headed coin, and a single tails would instantly drop C3 to 0 because P(tails | two-headed) = 0."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Continue the example: from the two-heads posteriors (0.1379, 0.3103, 0.5517), the third flip is tails. Compute the new posteriors. 2) Why does one tails set the two-headed coin''s posterior to exactly 0? 3) Confirm that updating with HH in one step (likelihoods 0.25, 0.5625, 1.0) gives the same result as two single-heads updates. Answers: 1) Likelihoods of tails: C1 = 0.5, C2 = 0.25, C3 = 0. Products: 0.1379 * 0.5 = 0.06897; 0.3103 * 0.25 = 0.07759; 0.5517 * 0 = 0. Total = 0.14655. Posteriors: C1 = 0.4706, C2 = 0.5294, C3 = 0. 2) Because P(tails | two-headed) = 0 makes its product 0, so its posterior is 0 / total = 0. 3) One-step products from a 1/3 prior: (1/3)*0.25 = 0.0833; (1/3)*0.5625 = 0.1875; (1/3)*1.0 = 0.3333; total 0.6042; posteriors 0.1379, 0.3103, 0.5517 - identical to the two-step result."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reusing the original prior for the second observation instead of the latest posterior. Mistake: forgetting to renormalize after each step. Exit: (a) In sequential updating, what plays the role of the prior on step two? (b) What happens to a hypothesis that assigns probability 0 to something you observe? Answers: the posterior from step one; its posterior becomes 0 and it is ruled out forever."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep a table with one row per hypothesis and columns prior, likelihood, prior*likelihood, posterior - the last column is the third divided by its own total.', 14),
  (7, 'The Beta-Binomial model', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Use a Beta distribution as a prior for an unknown probability, update it with binomial data by adding counts, and read off the posterior mean. Warm-up: if your prior is Beta(1, 1) and you see 1 success and 0 failures, what are the new parameters?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Suppose an unknown success probability p lies between 0 and 1 - a coin''s bias, a click-through rate, a cure rate. A Beta(a, b) distribution is a flexible prior over p; you can picture a as a count of prior successes and b as prior failures, so Beta(1, 1) is flat (all values of p equally likely) and larger a + b means a more confident, more concentrated prior. The data are binomial: k successes in n trials. The magic of conjugacy is that the posterior is again a Beta, with the counts simply added: posterior = Beta(a + k, b + n - k). The mean of Beta(a, b) is a / (a + b), so the posterior mean is (a + k) / (a + b + n). With a flat prior this is (k + 1) / (n + 2), Laplace''s rule of succession - close to the raw rate k/n but pulled gently toward 1/2, more so when n is small."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Start flat: prior Beta(1, 1), mean 1/2 = 0.5. Flip a coin 10 times and see 8 heads (k = 8, n = 10, so 2 failures). Posterior = Beta(1 + 8, 1 + 2) = Beta(9, 3). Posterior mean = 9 / (9 + 3) = 9/12 = 0.75. The raw rate was 8/10 = 0.80; the flat prior shrank it slightly toward 0.5. Now use an informative prior that already believes the coin is fair, Beta(50, 50), mean 0.5, and apply the same 8-of-10 data: posterior = Beta(58, 52), mean 58/110 = 0.527. The strong prior barely moved, because 10 flips are small next to 100 prior counts. Sequential check: updating Beta(1, 1) by 8 heads and 2 tails one flip at a time lands on Beta(9, 3) as well, since each success adds 1 to a and each failure adds 1 to b."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Prior Beta(2, 2); observe 3 successes and 1 failure. Give the posterior parameters and mean. 2) Prior Beta(1, 1); observe 0 successes in 5 trials. Give the posterior and mean, and compare to the raw rate 0/5. 3) A drug cures 7 of 10 patients under a flat prior; what posterior mean do you report? Answers: 1) Beta(2 + 3, 2 + 1) = Beta(5, 3), mean 5/8 = 0.625. 2) Beta(1, 6), mean 1/7 = 0.143; the raw rate is 0 but the rule of succession refuses to declare the cure impossible on 5 trials. 3) Beta(8, 4), mean 8/12 = 0.667."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: adding successes to b or failures to a - successes go to a, failures to b. Mistake: expecting the posterior mean to equal k/n exactly; it is pulled toward the prior mean, strongly when data are few. Exit: (a) After Beta(a, b) with k successes and f failures, what is the posterior? (b) What is the mean of Beta(9, 3)? Answers: Beta(a + k, b + f); 9/12 = 0.75."}]}'::jsonb, 'Write a Beta prior as Beta(a, b); after k successes in n trials the posterior is Beta(a + k, b + n - k). The Beta is ''conjugate'' to the binomial, so the update is pure arithmetic.', 15),
  (8, 'Continuous priors', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"State Bayes'' theorem for a continuous parameter, explain the role of the normalizing integral, and use proportionality to identify a posterior without doing the integral by hand. Warm-up: when the parameter can be any value in an interval, what replaces the sum in the denominator?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"When a hypothesis is a continuous value p, the prior is a density f(p) rather than a table of probabilities. Bayes'' theorem keeps its shape but the denominator becomes an integral: f(p|D) = P(D|p) * f(p) / P(D), where P(D) = integral of P(D|p) * f(p) dp taken over every possible p. The integral does exactly what the sum did in the discrete case: it adds up the numerator across all hypotheses so the posterior density integrates to 1. In practice you rarely grind through the integral, because posterior is proportional to likelihood times prior: f(p|D) ~ P(D|p) * f(p). If that product has the algebraic shape of a known distribution, you recognize it and the normalizer is whatever constant makes it a proper density. The Beta-Binomial model is exactly this trick: a Beta prior times a binomial likelihood is proportional to another Beta, so the update is just adding counts."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Take a flat prior on a coin''s bias, f(p) = 1 for p in [0, 1] (this is Beta(1, 1)). Observe 8 heads in 10 flips. The binomial likelihood is proportional to p^8 * (1 - p)^2. Multiply by the flat prior: f(p|D) ~ p^8 * (1 - p)^2 * 1 = p^8 * (1 - p)^2. Compare to a Beta(a, b) density, which is proportional to p^(a-1) * (1 - p)^(b-1). Matching exponents, a - 1 = 8 and b - 1 = 2, so a = 9 and b = 3: the posterior is Beta(9, 3) with mean 9/12 = 0.75 - the same result as the count-adding rule, now seen as pure proportionality. The normalizing integral, integral of p^8 * (1 - p)^2 dp from 0 to 1, evaluates to 1/2520, but you never needed its value to identify the shape."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) With a flat prior and 3 heads in 3 flips, write f(p|D) up to proportionality and name the Beta posterior. 2) Why does the exact value of the normalizing integral not affect which distribution the posterior is? 3) A Beta(2, 2) prior is proportional to p^1 * (1 - p)^1; multiply by a likelihood proportional to p^5 * (1 - p)^0 (5 successes, 0 failures) and name the posterior. Answers: 1) f(p|D) ~ p^3 * (1 - p)^0 = p^3, matching a - 1 = 3, b - 1 = 0, so Beta(4, 1). 2) The integral is a single constant that only rescales the whole density; it cannot change the p-dependence, which is what fixes the shape. 3) Product ~ p^6 * (1 - p)^1, so a - 1 = 6, b - 1 = 1: Beta(7, 2)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: trying to compute the normalizing integral before checking whether the shape is already a known distribution. Mistake: dropping the prior density when writing proportionality. Exit: (a) Write continuous Bayes'' theorem for a density f(p). (b) What is the posterior shape of a flat prior times a p^8 * (1 - p)^2 likelihood? Answers: f(p|D) = P(D|p) * f(p) / P(D) with P(D) an integral over p; Beta(9, 3)."}]}'::jsonb, 'For a continuous parameter, sums become integrals: the normalizer is P(D) = integral of P(D|p) * f(p) dp over all p, and the posterior density is f(p|D) = P(D|p) * f(p) / P(D).', 14),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"In sequential updating, the prior for the second observation is...","options":["the original prior again","the posterior from the first observation","the likelihood","always uniform"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Today''s posterior becomes tomorrow''s prior; that is the heart of sequential updating."},{"prompt":"Three equally likely coins (0.5, 0.75, 1.0) give one heads; the two-headed coin''s posterior is...","options":["1/3","0.4444","0.75","0.5"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Products (1/3)*{0.5,0.75,1.0} sum to 0.75; the two-headed coin''s 0.3333 / 0.75 = 0.4444."},{"prompt":"A hypothesis that assigns probability 0 to an observed outcome gets a posterior of...","options":["unchanged","0","1","0.5"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Its likelihood is 0, so its numerator is 0 and it is ruled out."},{"prompt":"With a Beta(a, b) prior and k successes, f failures, the posterior is...","options":["Beta(a + f, b + k)","Beta(a + k, b + f)","Beta(a - k, b - f)","Beta(k, f)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Successes add to a, failures add to b: Beta(a + k, b + f)."},{"prompt":"The mean of a Beta(9, 3) posterior is...","options":["0.80","0.75","0.50","0.90"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mean of Beta(a, b) is a / (a + b) = 9/12 = 0.75."},{"prompt":"For a continuous parameter, the normalizer P(D) is...","options":["a sum over two cases","an integral of P(D|p) * f(p) over all p","always 1","the prior mean"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sums become integrals: P(D) = integral of P(D|p) * f(p) dp, which makes the posterior integrate to 1."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Credible intervals vs confidence intervals', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Read a credible interval off a posterior, and say precisely how it differs in meaning from a frequentist confidence interval. Warm-up: after computing a posterior for p, what kind of interval can you extract from it directly?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Once you have a posterior distribution for a parameter, a credible interval is any range that contains a stated share of the posterior probability - a 95 percent credible interval is an interval [L, U] with P(L <= p <= U | data) = 0.95. A common choice is the central interval, cutting 2.5 percent off each tail. Because the Bayesian parameter has a distribution, the sentence ''there is a 95 percent probability the parameter lies in this interval'' is exactly correct. A frequentist confidence interval sounds similar but means something different: the parameter is a fixed unknown constant, and it is the interval that is random because it depends on the sample. A 95 percent confidence interval is produced by a procedure that, over many repeated samples, brackets the true value 95 percent of the time. For any single computed interval you cannot say the probability the parameter is inside is 0.95 - it either is or is not; 95 percent describes the method, not that one interval."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Suppose 10 flips give 8 heads under a flat prior, so the posterior is Beta(9, 3) with mean 0.75. Reading the central 95 percent region of this Beta gives roughly [0.482, 0.940]; the honest Bayesian statement is ''given the data, there is a 0.95 probability the bias lies between about 0.48 and 0.94.'' A frequentist analyzing the same 8-of-10 might report a confidence interval of similar width, but the licensed statement is ''if I repeated this experiment many times, 95 percent of the intervals I build this way would cover the true bias.'' Same data, two different sentences: the credible interval assigns probability to the parameter; the confidence interval assigns a success rate to the procedure."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) True or false: for a 95 percent confidence interval already computed from data, P(parameter is inside) = 0.95. 2) Which interval lets you legitimately say ''there is a 90 percent probability the rate is between 0.4 and 0.6''? 3) A wide credible interval signals what about your knowledge of the parameter? Answers: 1) False - the parameter is fixed and the interval is fixed; 95 percent describes the long-run procedure, not this one interval. 2) The credible interval, because it is a probability statement about the parameter given the data. 3) High remaining uncertainty; more or more informative data would tighten it."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: attaching ''probability the parameter is inside'' to a single confidence interval. Mistake: assuming a credible and a confidence interval always coincide - they can, especially with flat priors and large samples, but their meanings never merge. Exit: (a) What does a 95 percent credible interval let you say about the parameter? (b) What does the 95 percent in a confidence interval actually describe? Answers: that, given the data, there is a 0.95 probability the parameter lies in it; the long-run fraction of such intervals that cover the true value."}]}'::jsonb, 'A credible interval is a statement about the parameter given your data; a confidence interval is a statement about the long-run behavior of the procedure. The words ''probability that the parameter is in the interval'' are licensed only for the credible interval.', 13),
  (11, 'Bayesian vs frequentist thinking', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Contrast the two schools on what probability means, what gets a distribution, and how each answers a question, and pick the framing a problem calls for. Warm-up: in the Bayesian view, what object carries a probability distribution that the frequentist view treats as a fixed constant?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The frequentist reads probability as a long-run frequency: the chance of an event is the fraction of times it happens over many repetitions. Parameters are fixed unknown constants; the data are random; inference asks how the data behave assuming a hypothesis, via p-values and confidence intervals. The Bayesian reads probability as a degree of belief that can be updated with evidence. Parameters get probability distributions; you encode a prior, multiply by the likelihood, and report a posterior, credible intervals, and direct statements like P(hypothesis | data). Neither is universally right. The Bayesian approach shines when you have real prior information, when you update as data trickle in, or when you want a direct probability of a hypothesis. The frequentist approach is prized when priors are contentious and you want conclusions that lean only on the data-generating procedure. They also share machinery: both use the likelihood, and with a flat prior and plenty of data the numerical answers often nearly coincide, even though the sentences you may say about them differ."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Question: is a coin biased toward heads after 8 heads in 10 flips? A frequentist computes a p-value: the probability of seeing 8 or more heads in 10 flips if the coin were fair is about 0.055, so at the 0.05 level there is not quite enough evidence to reject fairness. A Bayesian with a flat prior reports the posterior Beta(9, 3), posterior mean 0.75, and can state P(p > 0.5 | data), which for this posterior is about 0.89 - a direct probability the coin favors heads. Same data: the frequentist answers ''the data are not extreme enough to reject a fair coin at 5 percent,'' while the Bayesian answers ''given the data, it is about 89 percent probable the coin favors heads.'' The two sentences are compatible; they answer different questions."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In which framework is the parameter random? 2) Which framework can report P(hypothesis | data) directly? 3) Give one situation where a frequentist analysis is preferable and one where a Bayesian analysis is. Answers: 1) The Bayesian framework treats the parameter as having a distribution; the frequentist treats it as a fixed constant and the data as random. 2) The Bayesian framework, through the posterior. 3) Frequentist: a regulated trial where any prior would be disputed and conclusions must rest on the procedure. Bayesian: updating a fraud-risk estimate as transactions arrive, where genuine prior information exists and you want a running probability."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading a p-value as the probability the hypothesis is true - it is the probability of data at least this extreme assuming the hypothesis. Mistake: assuming the two schools always disagree; with a flat prior and ample data the numbers frequently line up. Exit: (a) What does probability mean to a frequentist versus a Bayesian? (b) Which framework assigns a distribution to a parameter? Answers: a long-run frequency versus a degree of belief; the Bayesian framework."}]}'::jsonb, 'In the Bayesian view a parameter has a probability distribution; in the frequentist view a parameter is a fixed constant and probability lives in the data. Almost every difference in vocabulary flows from this one split.', 13),
  (12, 'Prior sensitivity', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Judge how strongly a prior influences the posterior, check robustness by trying several reasonable priors, and explain why data eventually overwhelm the prior. Warm-up: for a Beta(a, b) prior, what rough count of prior observations does a + b represent?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A prior is a modeling choice, so a careful analyst asks how much the conclusion depends on it. For the Beta-Binomial model the arithmetic makes this transparent: a Beta(a, b) prior acts like a + b prior observations, and the posterior mean (a + k) / (a + b + n) is a weighted blend of the prior mean a / (a + b) and the data rate k / n. When the real sample size n dwarfs a + b, the data dominate and the prior barely matters; when n is small relative to a + b, a strong prior can steer the result. Two practices follow. First, run a sensitivity check: recompute the posterior under a few defensible priors (flat, mildly skeptical, mildly optimistic) and see whether the decision changes. If it does not, the conclusion is robust; if it does, you have learned that you need more data or a more carefully justified prior. Second, remember that consistent priors wash out asymptotically: as more data arrive, posteriors from different reasonable priors converge, because a fixed a + b becomes negligible next to a growing n."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Data: 8 heads in 10 flips (k = 8, n = 10). Flat prior Beta(1, 1): posterior Beta(9, 3), mean 9/12 = 0.750. Skeptical fair-coin prior Beta(50, 50): posterior Beta(58, 52), mean 58/110 = 0.527. Here a + b = 100 swamps n = 10, so the skeptical prior barely moves and the two conclusions diverge - the analysis is not robust at this sample size. Now scale the data up tenfold to 80 heads in 100 flips (k = 80, n = 100). Flat prior: Beta(81, 21), mean 81/102 = 0.794. Same skeptical Beta(50, 50): posterior Beta(130, 70), mean 130/200 = 0.650, and pushing further to 800 of 1000 gives Beta(850, 250) with the flat prior (mean 0.773) versus Beta(850, 250) offset - the skeptical prior''s mean climbs to (50 + 800)/(100 + 1000) = 850/1100 = 0.773 as well. The gap between priors shrinks from 0.223 at n = 10 toward near-zero as n grows: the data have overwhelmed the prior."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) With k = 8, n = 10, compute the posterior mean under an optimistic Beta(8, 2) prior and compare it to the flat-prior 0.750. 2) Roughly how many prior observations does Beta(3, 7) represent, and what is its prior mean? 3) You get the same decision under flat, skeptical, and optimistic priors - what does that tell you? Answers: 1) Beta(8 + 8, 2 + 2) = Beta(16, 4), mean 16/20 = 0.800, a little above 0.750 because the optimistic prior leans high. 2) About 3 + 7 = 10 prior observations, prior mean 3/10 = 0.3. 3) The conclusion is robust to the prior, so the prior choice is not driving your result and you can report it with confidence."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: choosing a very strong prior by accident (large a + b) and then reading a barely-moved posterior as evidence the data agree with you. Mistake: reporting a single prior''s result without checking whether a different reasonable prior flips the decision. Exit: (a) In (a + k) / (a + b + n), what makes the data dominate the prior? (b) What is a sensitivity check? Answers: n being large relative to a + b; recomputing the posterior under several defensible priors to see if the conclusion holds."}]}'::jsonb, 'Think of a Beta(a, b) prior as carrying a + b ''pseudo-observations.'' Compare that number to the real sample size n to judge how much the prior can steer the posterior.', 14),
  (13, 'Final quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The posterior mean of a Beta(a, b) prior after k successes and f failures is...","options":["k / n","(a + k) / (a + b + k + f)","a / (a + b)","(a + b) / n"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Add counts to get Beta(a + k, b + f); its mean is (a + k) / (a + b + k + f)."},{"prompt":"In the 1 percent disease example, the posterior stayed low mainly because...","options":["the test was inaccurate","the prior (base rate) was very small","the likelihood was 0","the sample was large"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tiny prior means false positives from the healthy majority outweigh true positives, keeping P(H|D) around 16.7 percent."},{"prompt":"You may correctly say ''there is a 95 percent probability the parameter is in this interval'' for a...","options":["confidence interval","credible interval","p-value","point estimate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only the credible interval is a probability statement about the parameter given the data."},{"prompt":"A hypothesis giving probability 0 to observed data receives a posterior of...","options":["0","0.5","1","unchanged"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Its likelihood is 0, so it is ruled out entirely."},{"prompt":"A Beta prior is convenient with binomial data because it is...","options":["always flat","conjugate, so the posterior is another Beta","the same as a normal distribution","free of parameters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Conjugacy means Beta times binomial is proportional to a Beta, so updating just adds counts."},{"prompt":"As the sample size grows, posteriors from different reasonable priors...","options":["diverge further","converge toward each other","become the prior","become uniform"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fixed a + b becomes negligible next to a large n, so the data dominate and priors wash out."},{"prompt":"A p-value is the probability of...","options":["the hypothesis being true","data at least this extreme assuming the hypothesis","the parameter lying in an interval","a prior belief"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A p-value is computed assuming the hypothesis and measures how extreme the data are, not the probability the hypothesis is true."},{"prompt":"Given a flat prior and 8 heads in 10 flips, the posterior is Beta(9, 3); its mean is...","options":["0.80","0.75","0.50","0.90"],"answer":1,"explanation":"9 / (9 + 3) = 0.75, pulled slightly toward 0.5 from the raw 0.80 by the flat prior."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (14, 'Capstone: estimate a rate by Bayesian updating', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"The task","text":"Pick a real yes/no question you can gather data on and estimate its underlying rate p with a full Bayesian analysis. Good candidates: the fraction of free-throw attempts a player makes, the click rate on a link, the share of a bag of candy that is one color, or the germination rate of seeds. State the question, choose and justify a prior, collect n trials with k successes, compute the posterior, report a point estimate and a 95 percent credible interval, and finish with a sensitivity check."},{"heading":"The structure to follow","text":"1) State the question and define what a ''success'' means so p is unambiguous. 2) Choose a Beta(a, b) prior and justify it in one sentence, noting the a + b pseudo-observations it carries; if you have no information, a flat Beta(1, 1) is honest. 3) Record the data as k successes in n trials. 4) Compute the posterior Beta(a + k, b + n - k) and its mean (a + k) / (a + b + n). 5) Report a central 95 percent credible interval and state its meaning in plain words. 6) Sensitivity check: recompute the posterior mean under at least one other defensible prior and say whether your conclusion holds."},{"heading":"A fully worked model (of a different question)","text":"Question: what fraction of a large jar of mixed nuts are almonds? Success = ''the nut is an almond.'' Prior: no strong information, so Beta(1, 1), a flat prior carrying 2 pseudo-observations. Data: I scoop out n = 40 nuts and find k = 12 almonds. Posterior: Beta(1 + 12, 1 + 28) = Beta(13, 29). Posterior mean = 13 / (13 + 29) = 13/42 = 0.310. The raw rate is 12/40 = 0.300; the flat prior nudged it barely toward 0.5. Central 95 percent credible interval for Beta(13, 29) is roughly [0.183, 0.451], read as ''given my 40-nut sample, there is a 0.95 probability the almond fraction is between about 0.18 and 0.45.'' Sensitivity check: a mildly skeptical Beta(5, 5) prior (10 pseudo-observations, prior mean 0.5) gives posterior Beta(17, 33), mean 17/50 = 0.340 - close to 0.310, and the intervals overlap heavily, so the conclusion is robust to the prior at this sample size. Use this as a template of rigor and tone; do not copy the numbers, run your own question."},{"heading":"Self-check rubric","text":"Check yes to each: the question and the meaning of ''success'' are stated before any math; the prior is named as Beta(a, b) and justified, with its a + b pseudo-observations noted; the data appear as explicit k and n; the posterior is written as Beta(a + k, b + n - k) with its mean computed as (a + k) / (a + b + n); a 95 percent credible interval is reported and explained as a probability statement about p given the data; a sensitivity check recomputes under at least one other prior and states whether the conclusion holds. If all six are true, you have completed a valid Bayesian analysis end to end."}]}'::jsonb, null, 16)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0001-0000-4000-8000-000000000013' and l.position = v.pos
);
